window.SUMMIT_READ&&window.SUMMIT_READ("p3",{"part":"<p>Part III turns mathematics into physics and engineering. Dynamics in rotating frames, the laws of thermodynamics, chemical equilibrium and kinetics, fluid mechanics, heat transfer, solid mechanics, electromagnetism and feedback control are the eight pillars on which every aerospace discipline stands.</p>","ch":{"15":{"intro":"","secs":{"15.1":"<p>Newton’s laws hold in <em>inertial</em> frames. Vehicles are described in <em>body</em> frames that rotate. If frame <span class=\"mi\">B</span> rotates relative to inertial frame <span class=\"mi\">I</span> with angular velocity <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{\\omega}</span>, then for any vector <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{A}</span>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Transport theorem:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\left( \\frac{d\\mathbf{A}}{dt} \\right)_{I} = \\left( \\frac{d\\mathbf{A}}{dt} \\right)_{B} + \\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{A}</span>.</p></div>","15.2":"<p>Applying the theorem twice to the position <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{r}</span> of a particle (frames sharing an origin):</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\mathbf{a}_{I} = \\mathbf{a}_{B} + 2\\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{v}_{B} + \\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\left( \\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{r} \\right) + \\dot{\\mathbf{\\omega}} \\times \\mathbf{r}.</span></p><p>An observer in the rotating frame sees apparent forces: <em>Coriolis</em> <span class=\"mi\">- 2m\\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{v}_{B}</span>, <em>centrifugal</em> <span class=\"mi\">- m\\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\left( \\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{r} \\right)</span> and <em>Euler</em> <span class=\"mi\">- m\\dot{\\mathbf{\\omega}} \\times \\mathbf{r}</span>. Earth rotates at <span class=\"mi\">\\omega_{\\oplus} = 7.2921159 \\times 10^{- 5}</span> rad/s, giving the equatorial surface 465.1 m/s eastward. A site at latitude <span class=\"mi\">\\phi</span> moves at <span class=\"mi\">465.1\\cos\\phi</span> m/s, the free velocity gained by launching east. The centrifugal term makes effective gravity 9.780 m/s² at the equator and 9.832 m/s² at the poles. Coriolis deflection matters for long-duration atmospheric flight and for trajectory simulation in Earth-fixed coordinates (Chapter 31).</p>","15.3":"<p>For any system, the total external force accelerates the center of mass: <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{F}_{ext} = M\\mathbf{a}_{cm}</span>. The angular momentum about the center of mass, <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{H}_{cm} = \\sum\\mathbf{r}_{i}' \\times m_{i}\\mathbf{v}_{i}'</span> (primes relative to the CM), obeys <span class=\"mi\">{\\dot{\\mathbf{H}}}_{cm} = \\mathbf{M}_{cm,ext}</span>. Kinetic energy splits as <span class=\"mi\">T = \\frac{1}{2}Mv_{cm}^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sum m_{i}\\left| \\mathbf{v}_{i}' \\right|^{2}</span> (König’s theorem). Internal forces cancel in pairs and cannot change total momentum.</p>","15.4":"<p>Points of a rigid body move as <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{v}_{P} = \\mathbf{v}_{O} + \\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{r}_{P/O}</span>. The angular momentum about the CM is <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{H} = \\mathbf{I\\omega}</span>, with the inertia tensor</p><p><span class=\"md\">I_{ij} = \\int\\left( r^{2}\\delta_{ij} - x_{i}x_{j} \\right)dm,\\quad\\quad\\text{e.g. }I_{xx} = \\int\\left( y^{2} + z^{2} \\right)\\, dm,\\quad I_{xy} = - \\int xy\\, dm.</span></p><p><strong>Parallel-axis theorem:</strong> about a point displaced by <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{d}</span> from the CM, <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{I}_{O} = \\mathbf{I}_{cm} + m\\left( d^{2}\\mathbf{E} - \\mathbf{dd}^{T} \\right)</span>. <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{I}</span> is symmetric positive semi-definite; its eigenvectors are the <em>principal axes</em>, where the products of inertia vanish. Rotational kinetic energy is <span class=\"mi\">T_{rot} = \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{\\omega}^{T}\\mathbf{I\\omega}</span>.</p>","15.5":"<p>Differentiating <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{H} = \\mathbf{I\\omega}</span> in body axes (where <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{I}</span> is constant) with the transport theorem gives <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{I}\\dot{\\mathbf{\\omega}} + \\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{I\\omega} = \\mathbf{M}</span>. In principal axes:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">I_{1}{\\dot{\\omega}}_{1} - \\left( I_{2} - I_{3} \\right)\\omega_{2}\\omega_{3} = M_{1}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad I_{2}{\\dot{\\omega}}_{2} - \\left( I_{3} - I_{1} \\right)\\omega_{3}\\omega_{1} = M_{2}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad I_{3}{\\dot{\\omega}}_{3} - \\left( I_{1} - I_{2} \\right)\\omega_{1}\\omega_{2} = M_{3}</span>.</p></div><p><em>Torque-free axisymmetric body</em> (<span class=\"mi\">I_{1} = I_{2}</span>): <span class=\"mi\">\\omega_{3}</span> is constant, and the transverse angular velocity rotates in the body frame at <span class=\"mi\">\\lambda = \\left( I_{3} - I_{1} \\right)\\omega_{3}/I_{1}</span>; the body <em>nutates</em> about the fixed <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{H}</span>. <em>Stability of pure spin:</em> for a rigid body, spin about the axis of maximum or minimum inertia is stable, while spin about the intermediate axis is unstable (the tennis-racket or Dzhanibekov effect). With internal energy dissipation, only the <em>maximum</em>-inertia axis is stable (Chapter 32).</p>","15.6":"<p>Choose independent generalized coordinates <span class=\"mi\">q_{k}</span> that satisfy all constraints automatically. With <span class=\"mi\">L = T - V</span>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Lagrange’s equations:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{d}{dt}\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial{\\dot{q}}_{k}} - \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial q_{k}} = Q_{k}</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">Q_{k}</span> are generalized non-conservative forces obtained from virtual work, <span class=\"mi\">\\delta W = \\sum Q_{k}\\delta q_{k}</span>.</p></div><p>Example: a pendulum with <span class=\"mi\">T = \\frac{1}{2}mL^{2}{\\dot{\\theta}}^{2}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">V = - mgL\\cos\\theta</span> gives <span class=\"mi\">\\ddot{\\theta} + (g/L)\\sin\\theta = 0</span>, with small-angle period <span class=\"mi\">2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}</span>. If <span class=\"mi\">L</span> does not depend on some <span class=\"mi\">q_{k}</span> (a <em>cyclic</em> coordinate), its momentum <span class=\"mi\">p_{k} = \\partial L/\\partial{\\dot{q}}_{k}</span> is conserved. <em>Noether’s theorem:</em> every continuous symmetry implies a conservation law (time invariance gives energy; rotational invariance gives angular momentum). The <em>Hamiltonian</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\mathcal{H} = \\sum p_{k}{\\dot{q}}_{k} - L</span> leads to Hamilton’s equations. Hamilton’s principle (stationary action) underlies finite elements (Chapter 33) and optimal control (Chapter 46).</p>","15.7":"<p>Apply momentum conservation to a control volume that encloses the vehicle, with the exhaust crossing its boundary (Chapter 26). The result for translation is</p><p><span class=\"md\">m\\frac{d\\mathbf{v}}{dt} = \\mathbf{F}_{thrust} + \\mathbf{F}_{aero} + m\\mathbf{g},\\quad\\quad\\mathbf{F}_{thrust} = - \\dot{m}\\mathbf{c}_{rel} + \\left( p_{e} - p_{a} \\right)A_{e}{\\widehat{\\mathbf{n}}}_{e}.</span></p><p>Thrust thus behaves as an external force. For rotation, exhaust leaving with the vehicle’s angular velocity removes angular momentum. This produces <em>jet damping</em>, a small damping moment proportional to <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{m}</span> times the square of the distance from the CM to the nozzle exit. Internal relative motion (propellant slosh, gimballing engines) adds further terms that are modeled explicitly in launch-vehicle dynamics (Chapter 32).</p>"}},"16":{"intro":"","secs":{"16.1":"<p>A <em>closed system</em> exchanges energy but not mass; a <em>control volume</em> exchanges both. Properties are <em>intensive</em> (independent of amount: <span class=\"mi\">T</span>, <span class=\"mi\">p</span>, specific volume <span class=\"mi\">v = 1/\\rho</span>) or <em>extensive</em> (<span class=\"mi\">V</span>, <span class=\"mi\">U</span>). The <em>state postulate</em>: the state of a simple compressible substance is fixed by two independent intensive properties. A <em>quasi-static</em> process passes through equilibrium states. The <em>zeroth law</em> (two bodies in equilibrium with a third are in equilibrium with each other) makes temperature measurable.</p>","16.2":"<p>Closed system: <span class=\"mi\">dE = \\delta Q - \\delta W</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">E</span> includes internal, kinetic and potential energy, <span class=\"mi\">Q</span> is heat added and <span class=\"mi\">W</span> is work done by the system. Quasi-static boundary work is <span class=\"mi\">\\delta W = p\\, dV</span>. Define the <em>enthalpy</em> <span class=\"mi\">h = u + pv</span>. For a control volume in steady flow:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Steady-flow energy equation:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{Q} - {\\dot{W}}_{s} = \\dot{m}\\left\\lbrack \\left( h_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}V_{2}^{2} + gz_{2} \\right) - \\left( h_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}V_{1}^{2} + gz_{1} \\right) \\right\\rbrack</span>. <strong>Stagnation enthalpy</strong> <span class=\"mi\">h_{0} = h + \\frac{1}{2}V^{2}</span> is conserved in adiabatic flow with no shaft work, <em>even through shocks and friction</em>. A nozzle therefore converts enthalpy into kinetic energy: <span class=\"mi\">V_{e} = \\sqrt{2\\left( h_{0} - h_{e} \\right)}</span>.</p></div>","16.3":"<p>For an ideal gas, <span class=\"mi\">u</span> and <span class=\"mi\">h</span> depend on <span class=\"mi\">T</span> alone: <span class=\"mi\">c_{v} = du/dT</span>, <span class=\"mi\">c_{p} = dh/dT</span>, <span class=\"mi\">c_{p} - c_{v} = R</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma = c_{p}/c_{v}</span>, and so</p><p><span class=\"md\">c_{p} = \\frac{\\gamma R}{\\gamma - 1},\\quad\\quad c_{v} = \\frac{R}{\\gamma - 1}.</span></p><p>A <em>calorically perfect</em> gas has constant <span class=\"mi\">c_{p}</span>; a <em>thermally perfect</em> gas has <span class=\"mi\">c_{p}(T)</span>. Monatomic gases have <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma = 5/3</span>. Diatomic gases near room temperature have <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma = 7/5</span>, falling toward 9/7 as molecular vibration is excited above roughly 600–1000 K (Chapter 39). Rocket exhaust typically has <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma \\approx 1.15</span>–1.25.</p>","16.4":"<p>Isothermal: <span class=\"mi\">pv = \\text{const}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">W = RT\\ln\\left( v_{2}/v_{1} \\right)</span>. Isobaric: <span class=\"mi\">Q = \\Delta h</span>. Isochoric: <span class=\"mi\">Q = \\Delta u</span>. Polytropic: <span class=\"mi\">pv^{n} = \\text{const}</span>.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Isentropic relations (calorically perfect ideal gas):</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}} = \\left( \\frac{\\rho_{2}}{\\rho_{1}} \\right)^{\\gamma} = \\left( \\frac{T_{2}}{T_{1}} \\right)^{\\gamma/(\\gamma - 1)}</span>.</p></div>","16.5":"<p>Statements: no cyclic device can convert heat from a single reservoir entirely into work (Kelvin–Planck), and heat does not flow spontaneously from cold to hot (Clausius). Entropy is defined by <span class=\"mi\">dS = \\delta Q_{rev}/T</span>; for any process <span class=\"mi\">dS \\geq \\delta Q/T</span>, i.e. <span class=\"mi\">S_{gen} \\geq 0</span>. The <em>Gibbs equation</em> <span class=\"mi\">T\\, ds = du + p\\, dv = dh - v\\, dp</span> holds between any equilibrium states. For an ideal gas:</p><p><span class=\"md\">s_{2} - s_{1} = c_{p}\\ln\\frac{T_{2}}{T_{1}} - R\\ln\\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}.</span></p><p>An <em>isentropic</em> process is one that is both adiabatic and reversible. Sources of irreversibility: friction, heat transfer across finite temperature differences, mixing, unrestrained expansion, shock waves and chemical reaction away from equilibrium. The maximum efficiency between reservoirs at <span class=\"mi\">T_{H}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">T_{L}</span> is the Carnot value <span class=\"mi\">1 - T_{L}/T_{H}</span>. Since <span class=\"mi\">h_{0}</span> is conserved across an adiabatic loss, <span class=\"mi\">T_{0}</span> is unchanged while <span class=\"mi\">p_{0}</span> falls: <span class=\"mi\">p_{02}/p_{01} = e^{- \\Delta s/R}</span>. Stagnation-pressure loss is the measure of irreversibility in gas dynamics.</p>","16.6":"<p><strong>Ideal Brayton</strong> (isentropic compression, constant-pressure heating, isentropic expansion): <span class=\"mi\">\\eta = 1 - \\pi_{c}^{- (\\gamma - 1)/\\gamma}</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">\\pi_{c}</span> is the pressure ratio. Turbojets, ramjets and scramjets are Brayton cycles (Chapter 44). <strong>Humphrey</strong> (constant-volume heat addition) is more efficient at equal compression; this is the thermodynamic case for pressure-gain and detonation combustion (Chapter 47). <strong>A liquid rocket is an open cycle:</strong> propellants are pumped as liquids, which needs very little work because the pump work <span class=\"mi\">\\int v\\, dp</span> is small when <span class=\"mi\">v</span> is small; heat is added at high pressure in the chamber; and the gas expands in the nozzle. Pumping a liquid instead of compressing a gas is the thermodynamic reason rockets achieve high chamber pressures cheaply.</p>","16.7":"<p>Phase diagrams show solid, liquid and vapor regions, the saturation curve and the <em>critical point</em>, above which liquid and gas are indistinguishable (supercritical). Critical points: O₂ 154.6 K, 5.04 MPa; H₂ 33.2 K, 1.30 MPa; CH₄ 190.6 K, 4.60 MPa; N₂ 126.2 K, 3.40 MPa; H₂O 647.1 K, 22.06 MPa. Modern engine chambers often operate above the propellants’ critical pressures, which changes injection physics (Chapter 47). The compressibility factor <span class=\"mi\">Z = pv/(RT)</span> measures departure from ideality; cubic equations of state (van der Waals, Peng–Robinson) model it. The <em>Clausius–Clapeyron</em> relation <span class=\"mi\">dp_{sat}/dT = h_{fg}/\\left( Tv_{fg} \\right) \\approx p\\, h_{fg}/\\left( RT^{2} \\right)</span> governs boiling, cryogenic tank pressurization and boil-off.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Physical hazard</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Cryogenic liquids expand about 700 to 860 times in volume when they vaporize. A sealed container of cryogen becomes a pressure bomb, and nitrogen or helium boil-off in an enclosed room can cause asphyxiation without warning, because the body does not sense low oxygen.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> The same thermodynamics taught in this section (phase change, the Clausius–Clapeyron relation) is what makes these hazards real.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> University cryogenics and low-temperature laboratory courses; Compressed Gas Association (CGA) safety publications; institutional cryogen-handling training before using even liquid nitrogen in any laboratory.</p></aside>","16.8":"<p>Helmholtz <span class=\"mi\">a = u - Ts</span>; Gibbs <span class=\"mi\">g = h - Ts</span>. At fixed <span class=\"mi\">T</span> and <span class=\"mi\">p</span>, a system’s equilibrium state minimizes <span class=\"mi\">G</span>, the principle behind chemical-equilibrium calculations (Chapter 17). Maxwell relations follow from the equality of mixed partial derivatives, for example <span class=\"mi\">(\\partial s/\\partial p)_{T} = - (\\partial v/\\partial T)_{p}</span>. The chemical potential is <span class=\"mi\">\\mu_{i} = \\left( \\partial G/\\partial n_{i} \\right)_{T,p,n_{j \\neq i}}</span>; for an ideal-gas species, <span class=\"mi\">\\mu_{i} = \\mu_{i}^{\\circ}(T) + R_{u}T\\ln\\left( p_{i}/p^{\\circ} \\right)</span>.</p>"}},"17":{"intro":"","secs":{"17.1":"<p>Each species’ enthalpy combines chemical and sensible parts: <span class=\"mi\">h_{i}(T) = \\Delta h_{f,i}^{\\circ} + \\int_{298.15}^{T}c_{p,i}\\, dT</span>. Property data are tabulated as NASA polynomials (7 or 9 coefficients per temperature range) giving <span class=\"mi\">c_{p}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">h</span> and <span class=\"mi\">s^{\\circ}</span>; the JANAF tables are the classic source.</p>","17.2":"<p>At constant pressure with no heat loss and no work, the products’ absolute enthalpy equals the reactants’: <span class=\"mi\">\\sum_{R}^{}n_{i}h_{i}\\left( T_{in} \\right) = \\sum_{P}^{}n_{i}h_{i}\\left( T_{ad} \\right)</span>. Ignoring dissociation grossly overestimates <span class=\"mi\">T_{ad}</span> for energetic propellants. At high temperature the products partly dissociate into H, O, OH and similar species, absorbing energy (their formation enthalpies are positive), so the true <span class=\"mi\">T_{ad}</span> is found together with the equilibrium composition. Stoichiometric H₂/O₂ at 1 atm reaches about 3080 K; at rocket chamber pressures, about 3500–3600 K.</p>","17.3":"<div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Gibbs minimization.</strong> At fixed <span class=\"mi\">T</span> and <span class=\"mi\">p</span>, the equilibrium composition minimizes <span class=\"mi\">G = \\sum_{i}^{}n_{i}\\mu_{i}</span> subject to conservation of each element <span class=\"mi\">j</span>: <span class=\"mi\">\\sum_{i}^{}a_{ij}n_{i} = b_{j}</span>. With Lagrange multipliers <span class=\"mi\">\\lambda_{j}</span>: <span class=\"mi\">\\mu_{i} + \\sum_{j}^{}\\lambda_{j}a_{ij} = 0</span> for every species. This is the method of NASA’s CEA code. <strong>Single reaction</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\sum\\nu_{i}A_{i} = 0</span>: <span class=\"mi\">K_{p}(T) = \\prod_{i}^{}\\left( p_{i}/p^{\\circ} \\right)^{\\nu_{i}} = exp\\left( - \\Delta G^{\\circ}/R_{u}T \\right)</span>.</p></div><p>Because <span class=\"mi\">p_{i} = X_{i}p</span>, <span class=\"mi\">K_{p} = \\prod X_{i}^{\\nu_{i}}\\left( p/p^{\\circ} \\right)^{\\Delta\\nu}</span> with <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta\\nu = \\sum\\nu_{i}</span>. For dissociation (<span class=\"mi\">\\Delta\\nu &gt; 0</span>), raising <span class=\"mi\">p</span> lowers the dissociated fraction, quantifying Le Chatelier’s principle. The temperature dependence follows the van ’t Hoff equation, <span class=\"mi\">d\\ln K_{p}/dT = \\Delta H^{\\circ}/\\left( R_{u}T^{2} \\right)</span>.</p>","17.4":"<p>An elementary reaction <span class=\"mi\">\\sum\\nu_{i}'A_{i} \\rightleftharpoons \\sum\\nu_{i}''A_{i}</span> proceeds at net rate <span class=\"mi\">k_{f}\\prod\\left\\lbrack A_{i} \\right\\rbrack^{\\nu_{i}'} - k_{b}\\prod\\left\\lbrack A_{i} \\right\\rbrack^{\\nu_{i}''}</span> (law of mass action), with <span class=\"mi\">k_{b} = k_{f}/K_{c}</span> by detailed balance. Rate coefficients follow the <em>modified Arrhenius</em> form</p><p><span class=\"md\">k = AT^{n}\\exp\\left( - \\frac{E_{a}}{R_{u}T} \\right).</span></p><p>Third-body reactions (<span class=\"mi\">H + O_{2} + M \\rightarrow HO_{2} + M</span>) depend on total density and can show pressure fall-off. <em>Chain mechanisms</em> have initiation, propagation, branching (the key H₂/O₂ step <span class=\"mi\">H + O_{2} \\rightarrow OH + O</span> doubles the radicals) and termination. The competition between branching and termination sets the explosion limits and the <em>ignition delay</em>. Detailed mechanisms contain tens to thousands of reactions. They are stiff (Chapter 14) and are often reduced for CFD.</p>","17.5":"<p><strong>Premixed flames</strong> are self-propagating reaction–diffusion waves. Thermal theory gives the laminar flame speed <span class=\"mi\">S_{L} \\sim \\sqrt{\\alpha\\,\\omega/\\rho}</span> and the thickness <span class=\"mi\">\\delta \\sim \\alpha/S_{L}</span>: about 0.4 m/s for methane–air and 2–3 m/s for hydrogen–air. Mixtures burn only between flammability limits. <strong>Non-premixed (diffusion) flames</strong> are controlled by mixing. In the fast-chemistry (Burke–Schumann) limit they sit at the stoichiometric surface of the <em>mixture fraction</em> <span class=\"mi\">Z</span> (the local fraction of mass that came from the fuel stream), <span class=\"mi\">Z_{st} = 1/\\left\\lbrack 1 + (O/F)_{st} \\right\\rbrack</span>. Excessive strain extinguishes them. Rocket injectors produce mostly non-premixed or partially premixed combustion.</p>","17.6":"<p>A burning or evaporating droplet follows the <em>d² law</em>, <span class=\"mi\">d^{2} = d_{0}^{2} - Kt</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">K</span> of order 1 mm²/s, so its lifetime is <span class=\"mi\">d_{0}^{2}/K</span>. Halving the droplet size quarters the lifetime. Atomization quality therefore sets the chamber length needed for complete combustion (Chapter 27).</p>","17.7":"<p>A <em>detonation</em> is a shock wave sustained by the energy released just behind it. It travels supersonically relative to the reactants, typically 1.5–3 km/s in gases, and roughly doubles the pressure beyond what constant-volume combustion would give. A <em>deflagration</em> is subsonic and driven by diffusion. The Chapman–Jouguet (CJ) theory selects the detonation speed at which the products leave at their local sound speed. Accidental deflagration-to-detonation transition is a major hazard in propellant handling. Its controlled use powers rotating detonation engines (Chapter 47).</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Physical hazard and dual-use</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Fuel vapors and hydrogen–air or hydrogen–oxygen mixtures can transition from burning to detonation inside pipes, tanks and rooms, producing pressures far beyond what those structures were designed for.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Detonation physics is also the science of explosives. This document teaches the wave physics needed for propulsion and safety (Chapman–Jouguet theory, ZND structure, cell size; Chapter 47).</p><p><strong>Omitted here.</strong> Explosive compositions, initiation devices and charge design. They give weapons uplift, are tightly regulated, and are not needed to understand propulsion.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> Graduate combustion courses; university detonation-research laboratories operating under institutional safety review; the Combustion Institute’s journals and symposia; industrial process-safety and explosion-protection training (for example NFPA standards on explosion prevention).</p></aside>","17.8":"<p>Chapter 26 will show that the ideal exhaust velocity scales as <span class=\"mi\">\\sqrt{T_{c}\\mathcal{/M}}</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">T_{c}</span> is the chamber temperature and <span class=\"mi\">\\mathcal{M}</span> the product molar mass. Good propellants therefore release much energy per unit mass and form light products (H₂O, H₂ and CO rather than CO₂). Real choices also weigh density (smaller tanks), storability, ignition behavior, toxicity, cost and material compatibility.</p>"}},"18":{"intro":"","secs":{"18.1":"<p>A fluid is treated as a continuum when the molecular mean free path <span class=\"mi\">\\lambda</span> is much smaller than the flow scale <span class=\"mi\">L</span> (Knudsen number <span class=\"mi\">Kn = \\lambda/L \\ll 1</span>; Chapter 40 covers the alternative). A <em>Newtonian</em> fluid has shear stress proportional to strain rate: <span class=\"mi\">\\tau = \\mu\\, du/dy</span>, with dynamic viscosity <span class=\"mi\">\\mu</span> and kinematic viscosity <span class=\"mi\">\\nu = \\mu/\\rho</span>. Gas viscosity <em>increases</em> with temperature (molecules carry momentum farther); liquid viscosity decreases. For air, <em>Sutherland’s law</em> is</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\mu = \\frac{1.458 \\times 10^{- 6}\\, T^{3/2}}{T + 110.4}\\ \\ Pa\\, s\\quad\\left( T\\ \\text{in K} \\right).</span></p><p>Other properties: surface tension <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma</span> (atomization and capillarity) and bulk modulus <span class=\"mi\">K = \\rho\\,\\partial p/\\partial\\rho</span> (compressibility).</p>","18.2":"<p>At rest, <span class=\"mi\">\\nabla p = \\rho\\mathbf{g}</span>; vertically, <span class=\"mi\">dp/dz = - \\rho g</span>. In a tank accelerating at <span class=\"mi\">a</span> along its axis, the effective gravity is <span class=\"mi\">g + a</span>, so the pressure at depth <span class=\"mi\">h</span> below the liquid surface is <span class=\"mi\">p = p_{ullage} + \\rho(g + a)h</span>. This sets turbopump inlet conditions and structural loads (Chapters 27 and 33). Buoyancy equals the weight of displaced fluid (Archimedes).</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Atmosphere from first principles.</strong> Combine <span class=\"mi\">dp/dz = - \\rho g</span> with <span class=\"mi\">p = \\rho RT</span>: <span class=\"mi\">dp/p = - g\\, dz/(RT)</span>. Isothermal layer: <span class=\"mi\">p = p_{1}e^{- \\left( z - z_{1} \\right)/H}</span> with <em>scale height</em> <span class=\"mi\">H = RT/g</span> (about 7–8.5 km). Linear temperature layer <span class=\"mi\">T = T_{1} - L\\left( z - z_{1} \\right)</span>: <span class=\"mi\">p/p_{1} = \\left( T/T_{1} \\right)^{g/(RL)}</span>. The 1976 U.S. Standard Atmosphere (Appendix D) stacks such layers.</p></div>","18.3":"<p>The <em>Eulerian</em> description gives fields <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{u}\\left( \\mathbf{x},t \\right)</span> at fixed points; the <em>Lagrangian</em> description follows particles. The rate of change following a particle is the <em>material derivative</em> <span class=\"mi\">D/Dt = \\partial/\\partial t + \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\nabla</span>. Streamlines are everywhere tangent to <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{u}</span>; in steady flow they coincide with pathlines and streaklines. The velocity gradient splits into the <em>strain-rate tensor</em> <span class=\"mi\">S_{ij} = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( \\partial_{j}u_{i} + \\partial_{i}u_{j} \\right)</span> and rotation. <em>Vorticity</em> is <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{\\omega} = \\nabla \\times \\mathbf{u}</span>, and <em>circulation</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\Gamma = \\oint\\mathbf{u} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l} = \\iint\\mathbf{\\omega} \\cdot \\widehat{\\mathbf{n}}\\, dA</span>. <em>Kelvin’s theorem:</em> in inviscid, barotropic flow with conservative body forces, the circulation around a material loop is constant. Flows that start irrotational stay irrotational until viscosity, baroclinic effects or curved shocks (Chapter 36) generate vorticity.</p>","18.4":"<p>Applying the Reynolds transport theorem (Chapter 9) to a fixed control volume <span class=\"mi\">V</span> with surface <span class=\"mi\">S</span>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Mass:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{V}^{}\\rho\\, dV + \\oint_{S}^{}\\rho\\left( \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\widehat{\\mathbf{n}} \\right)\\, dA = 0</span>. <strong>Momentum:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{V}^{}\\rho\\mathbf{u}\\, dV + \\oint_{S}^{}\\rho\\mathbf{u}\\left( \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\widehat{\\mathbf{n}} \\right)\\, dA = \\oint_{S}^{}\\left( - p\\widehat{\\mathbf{n}} + \\mathbf{\\tau} \\cdot \\widehat{\\mathbf{n}} \\right)dA + \\int_{V}^{}\\rho\\mathbf{g}\\, dV + \\mathbf{F}_{ext}</span>. <strong>Energy:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{V}^{}\\rho e_{0}\\, dV + \\oint_{S}^{}\\rho h_{0}\\left( \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\widehat{\\mathbf{n}} \\right)\\, dA = \\dot{Q} - {\\dot{W}}_{s} + {\\dot{W}}_{visc}</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">e_{0} = e + \\frac{1}{2}u^{2}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">h_{0} = h + \\frac{1}{2}u^{2}</span>.</p></div><p>Control-volume analysis gives forces without knowing interior details: jet impingement, nozzle thrust (Chapter 26) and the drag of a body from a wake survey.</p>","18.5":"<p>Integrating the momentum equation along a streamline for steady, inviscid, incompressible flow with no shaft work or heat addition gives <span class=\"mi\">p + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^{2} + \\rho gz = \\text{const}</span> along that streamline (and everywhere, if the flow is also irrotational). A Pitot-static probe then gives <span class=\"mi\">V = \\sqrt{2\\left( p_{0} - p \\right)/\\rho}</span>.</p><div class=\"pit\"><p><strong>When Bernoulli fails.</strong> Across streamlines in rotational flow; in viscous regions (boundary layers, wakes); through shocks; across pumps, turbines and heat addition; in unsteady flow (add <span class=\"mi\">\\rho\\,\\partial\\phi/\\partial t</span>); for <span class=\"mi\">M \\gtrsim 0.3</span> (use the compressible form of Chapter 23). The popular “equal transit time” explanation of lift is false; lift follows from circulation (Chapter 25).</p></div>","18.6":"<p>Shrinking the control volume to a point gives</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{\\partial\\rho}{\\partial t} + \\nabla \\cdot \\left( \\rho\\mathbf{u} \\right) = 0,\\quad\\quad\\rho\\frac{D\\mathbf{u}}{Dt} = - \\nabla p + \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{\\tau} + \\rho\\mathbf{g}.</span></p><p>For a Newtonian fluid with Stokes’ hypothesis, <span class=\"mi\">\\tau_{ij} = \\mu\\left( \\partial_{j}u_{i} + \\partial_{i}u_{j} \\right) - \\frac{2}{3}\\mu\\left( \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\right)\\delta_{ij}</span>. For constant <span class=\"mi\">\\rho</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\mu</span>, these become the incompressible Navier–Stokes equations:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0,\\quad\\quad\\rho\\left( \\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{u}}{\\partial t} + \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\nabla\\mathbf{u} \\right) = - \\nabla p + \\mu\\nabla^{2}\\mathbf{u} + \\rho\\mathbf{g}</span>.</p></div><p>The energy equation in enthalpy form is <span class=\"mi\">\\rho\\, Dh/Dt = Dp/Dt + \\nabla \\cdot (k\\nabla T) + \\Phi</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">\\Phi \\geq 0</span> is viscous dissipation. Exact solutions: <em>Couette</em> flow (linear profile between sliding plates); <em>Poiseuille</em> flow (parabolic profile in a pipe or channel); <em>Stokes’ first problem</em>, a plate started impulsively, with <span class=\"mi\">u = U\\, erfc\\left\\lbrack y/\\left( 2\\sqrt{\\nu t} \\right) \\right\\rbrack</span>, showing that vorticity diffuses a distance <span class=\"mi\">\\sim \\sqrt{\\nu t}</span>.</p>","18.7":"<p>Nondimensionalizing the governing equations exposes the controlling groups: Reynolds <span class=\"mi\">Re = \\rho VL/\\mu</span> (inertia/viscous); Mach <span class=\"mi\">M = V/a</span> (compressibility); Froude <span class=\"mi\">Fr = V/\\sqrt{gL}</span> (inertia/gravity; free surfaces, slosh); Strouhal <span class=\"mi\">St = fL/V</span> (unsteadiness); Prandtl <span class=\"mi\">\\Pr = \\mu c_{p}/k</span> (momentum/thermal diffusion); Weber <span class=\"mi\">We = \\rho V^{2}L/\\sigma</span> (inertia/surface tension; atomization); Knudsen <span class=\"mi\">Kn</span> (rarefaction); Eckert <span class=\"mi\">Ec = V^{2}/\\left( c_{p}\\Delta T \\right)</span> (viscous heating). <em>Complete similarity</em> requires every relevant group to match between model and prototype. Usually only <em>partial similarity</em> is achievable, and choosing which groups to match is an engineering judgment (Chapter 49).</p>","18.8":"<p>Laminar pipe flow (Hagen–Poiseuille): <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta p = 128\\mu LQ/\\left( \\pi D^{4} \\right)</span>, valid for <span class=\"mi\">Re_{D} &lt; 2300</span>. In general, <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta p = f\\,(L/D)\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^{2}</span> (Darcy–Weisbach), with <span class=\"mi\">f = 64/Re</span> (laminar). For turbulent flow the Haaland approximation to Colebrook gives</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{f}} = - 1.8\\log_{10}\\left\\lbrack \\left( \\frac{\\varepsilon/D}{3.7} \\right)^{1.11} + \\frac{6.9}{Re_{D}} \\right\\rbrack,</span></p><p>where <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon</span> is wall roughness. Fittings add losses <span class=\"mi\">K\\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^{2}</span>. Noncircular passages, such as cooling channels, use the hydraulic diameter <span class=\"mi\">D_{h} = 4A/P</span>.</p>","18.9":"<p>At high <span class=\"mi\">Re</span>, viscous effects are confined to thin layers near walls. Over a flat plate (zero pressure gradient):</p><div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Quantity</th> <th>Laminar (Blasius)</th> <th>Turbulent (power law)</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Thickness <span class=\"mi\">\\delta_{99}/x</span></td> <td><span class=\"md\">5.0/\\sqrt{Re_{x}}</span></td> <td><span class=\"md\">0.37/Re_{x}^{1/5}</span></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Local skin friction <span class=\"mi\">c_{f}</span></td> <td><span class=\"md\">0.664/\\sqrt{Re_{x}}</span></td> <td><span class=\"md\">0.0592/Re_{x}^{1/5}</span></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Average over length <span class=\"mi\">L</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{F}</span></td> <td><span class=\"md\">1.328/\\sqrt{Re_{L}}</span></td> <td><span class=\"md\">0.074/Re_{L}^{1/5}</span></td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>Transition on a smooth flat plate in a quiet stream occurs at roughly <span class=\"mi\">Re_{x} \\approx 5 \\times 10^{5}</span>, but it is highly sensitive to disturbances (Chapter 37). An <em>adverse</em> pressure gradient (<span class=\"mi\">dp/dx &gt; 0</span>) decelerates the near-wall fluid until the layer <em>separates</em>, producing form drag; streamlining delays separation. The sudden drag drop of spheres near <span class=\"mi\">Re \\approx 3 \\times 10^{5}</span> (the drag crisis) occurs because a turbulent boundary layer resists separation better.</p>","18.10":"<p>Turbulence is unsteady, three-dimensional, vortical, strongly diffusive and dissipative over a wide range of scales. Decompose <span class=\"mi\">u = \\bar{u} + u'</span>. Averaging the momentum equation produces the <em>Reynolds stresses</em> <span class=\"mi\">- \\rho\\overline{u_{i}'u_{j}'}</span>, unknowns that must be modeled (the <em>closure problem</em>; Chapter 37). Energy cascades from large eddies (size <span class=\"mi\">L</span>, velocity <span class=\"mi\">u</span>, dissipation rate <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon \\sim u^{3}/L</span>) to the <em>Kolmogorov scales</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\eta = \\left( \\nu^{3}/\\varepsilon \\right)^{1/4}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\tau_{\\eta} = (\\nu/\\varepsilon)^{1/2}</span>, where viscosity converts kinetic energy to heat. Since <span class=\"mi\">L/\\eta \\sim Re^{3/4}</span>, resolving all scales (direct numerical simulation) costs roughly <span class=\"mi\">Re^{3}</span> operations.</p>"}},"19":{"intro":"","secs":{"19.1":"<p>Heat flux is <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{q} = - k\\nabla T</span>. Representative conductivities (W/(m·K)): copper alloys 300–400; aluminum 120–237; titanium alloys 7; nickel superalloys 11–25; carbon–carbon 5–50 (direction-dependent); silica reusable tile about 0.05; still air 0.026. Energy conservation gives the <em>heat equation</em></p><p><span class=\"md\">\\rho c\\frac{\\partial T}{\\partial t} = \\nabla \\cdot (k\\nabla T) + \\dot{q}''',\\quad\\quad\\alpha = \\frac{k}{\\rho c}\\ \\text{(thermal diffusivity)}.</span></p>","19.2":"<p>Plane wall: <span class=\"mi\">q = k\\Delta T/L</span>. Thermal resistances add like electrical ones: wall <span class=\"mi\">L/(kA)</span>; cylinder <span class=\"mi\">\\ln\\left( r_{2}/r_{1} \\right)/(2\\pi kL)</span>; convection <span class=\"mi\">1/(hA)</span>; contact <span class=\"mi\">R''_{c}/A</span>. <em>Fins</em> extend area; a uniform fin with an adiabatic tip has efficiency <span class=\"mi\">\\eta_{f} = tanh(mL)/(mL)</span> with <span class=\"mi\">m = \\sqrt{hP/\\left( kA_{c} \\right)}</span>.</p>","19.3":"<p>The <em>Biot number</em> <span class=\"mi\">Bi = hL_{c}/k</span> (<span class=\"mi\">L_{c} = V/A_{s}</span>) compares internal to surface resistance. If <span class=\"mi\">Bi &lt; 0.1</span>, the body is nearly isothermal (<em>lumped capacitance</em>): <span class=\"mi\">T - T_{\\infty} = \\left( T_{0} - T_{\\infty} \\right)e^{- t/\\tau}</span> with <span class=\"mi\">\\tau = \\rho cV/\\left( hA_{s} \\right)</span>. Otherwise solve the heat equation. At early times (<span class=\"mi\">Fo = \\alpha t/L^{2} \\lesssim 0.1</span>) every body behaves as a semi-infinite solid (Chapter 12); at later times use one-term series solutions.</p>","19.4":"<p>Newton’s law <span class=\"mi\">q = h\\left( T_{w} - T_{ref} \\right)</span> defines the heat-transfer coefficient <span class=\"mi\">h</span>. At low speed <span class=\"mi\">T_{ref}</span> is the free-stream temperature; at high speed it is the <em>adiabatic wall temperature</em> <span class=\"mi\">T_{aw}</span> (Chapter 37), so that <span class=\"mi\">q = h\\left( T_{aw} - T_{w} \\right)</span>. Dimensionless forms: Nusselt <span class=\"mi\">Nu = hL/k</span>; Prandtl <span class=\"mi\">\\Pr = \\nu/\\alpha</span> (air 0.71, water about 7, liquid metals about 0.01); Stanton <span class=\"mi\">St = h/\\left( \\rho uc_{p} \\right) = Nu/\\left( Re\\,\\Pr \\right)</span>.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Configuration</th> <th>Correlation</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Flat plate, laminar (local)</td> <td><span class=\"md\">Nu_{x} = 0.332\\, Re_{x}^{1/2}Pr^{1/3}</span></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Flat plate, turbulent (local)</td> <td><span class=\"md\">Nu_{x} = 0.0296\\, Re_{x}^{4/5}Pr^{1/3}</span></td> </tr> <tr> <td>Pipe, laminar, fully developed</td> <td><span class=\"mi\">Nu_{D} = 3.66</span> (uniform <span class=\"mi\">T_{w}</span>), <span class=\"mi\">4.36</span> (uniform <span class=\"mi\">q</span>)</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Pipe, turbulent (Dittus–Boelter)</td> <td><span class=\"mi\">Nu_{D} = 0.023\\, Re_{D}^{0.8}Pr^{n}</span>; <span class=\"mi\">n = 0.4</span> heating, <span class=\"mi\">0.3</span> cooling the fluid</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div>","19.5":"<p>Momentum and heat are carried across a boundary layer by the same molecular and turbulent mixing. For <span class=\"mi\">\\Pr = 1</span>, <span class=\"mi\">St = c_{f}/2</span>. More generally:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Chilton–Colburn analogy:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">St\\, Pr^{2/3} = c_{f}/2</span>. Heating can be predicted from skin friction and vice versa. This is the central link in aerothermodynamics (Chapter 41): wherever friction is high, heating is high.</p></div>","19.6":"<p>The gas-side heat-transfer coefficient in chambers and nozzles is estimated by the <em>Bartz correlation</em>:</p><p><span class=\"md\">h_{g} = \\frac{0.026}{D_{t}^{0.2}}\\left( \\frac{\\mu^{0.2}c_{p}}{Pr^{0.6}} \\right)_{0}\\left( \\frac{p_{c}}{c^{*}} \\right)^{0.8}\\left( \\frac{D_{t}}{r_{c}} \\right)^{0.1}\\left( \\frac{A_{t}}{A} \\right)^{0.9}\\sigma,</span></p><p><span class=\"md\">\\sigma = \\left\\lbrack \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{T_{w}}{T_{c}}\\left( 1 + \\frac{\\gamma - 1}{2}M^{2} \\right) + \\frac{1}{2} \\right\\rbrack^{- 0.68}\\left\\lbrack 1 + \\frac{\\gamma - 1}{2}M^{2} \\right\\rbrack^{- 0.12},</span></p><p>where <span class=\"mi\">D_{t}</span> is the throat diameter, <span class=\"mi\">r_{c}</span> the throat radius of curvature, <span class=\"mi\">c^{*}</span> the characteristic velocity (Chapter 26), and subscript 0 denotes chamber stagnation properties. Because <span class=\"mi\">h_{g} \\propto \\left( p_{c}/c^{*} \\right)^{0.8}\\left( A_{t}/A \\right)^{0.9}</span>, i.e. roughly to the 0.8 power of the local mass flux, the heat flux peaks at the throat and rises nearly linearly with chamber pressure. This is the central challenge of high-pressure engines (Chapter 27).</p>","19.7":"<p>A blackbody emits <span class=\"mi\">E_{b} = \\sigma T^{4}</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma = 5.670374 \\times 10^{- 8}</span> W/(m²·K⁴), with spectral peak at <span class=\"mi\">\\lambda_{\\max}T = 2897.8</span> μm·K (Wien). Real surfaces emit <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon\\sigma T^{4}</span>. <em>Kirchhoff’s law</em>: spectral absorptivity equals spectral emissivity, and for a gray surface <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha = \\varepsilon</span>. The <em>view factor</em> <span class=\"mi\">F_{ij}</span> is the fraction of radiation leaving surface <span class=\"mi\">i</span> that strikes <span class=\"mi\">j</span>; reciprocity gives <span class=\"mi\">A_{i}F_{ij} = A_{j}F_{ji}</span> and summation gives <span class=\"mi\">\\sum_{j}^{}F_{ij} = 1</span>. Two large parallel gray plates exchange <span class=\"mi\">q = \\sigma\\left( T_{1}^{4} - T_{2}^{4} \\right)/\\left( 1/\\varepsilon_{1} + 1/\\varepsilon_{2} - 1 \\right)</span>.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Radiative equilibrium.</strong> If a surface rejects all incoming convective flux by radiation to cold surroundings (no conduction inward): <span class=\"mi\">q_{conv} = \\varepsilon\\sigma T_{w}^{4} \\Rightarrow T_{w} = \\left( q_{conv}/\\varepsilon\\sigma \\right)^{1/4}</span>. High emissivity lowers the wall temperature, the principle of radiatively cooled nozzle extensions and reusable thermal protection (Chapter 42).</p></div><p>Gases such as H₂O and CO₂ emit and absorb in bands, and soot radiates as a near-continuum. Chamber, plume and high-speed shock-layer radiation (Chapter 41) require <em>participating-media</em> methods.</p>","19.8":"<p>As wall superheat rises, a liquid moves from natural convection through <em>nucleate boiling</em> (very high <span class=\"mi\">h</span>) to the <em>critical heat flux</em> (CHF). Beyond CHF a vapor film insulates the wall (film boiling) and the wall temperature jumps, possibly to failure (“burnout”). Regenerative cooling with subcritical coolant must remain below CHF. Coolants above their critical pressure do not boil, but their properties vary sharply near the pseudo-critical temperature, which can cause heat-transfer deterioration.</p>","19.9":"<p>With capacity rates <span class=\"mi\">C = \\dot{m}c_{p}</span>, the effectiveness–NTU method gives <span class=\"mi\">q = \\varepsilon_{hx}C_{\\min}\\left( T_{h,in} - T_{c,in} \\right)</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon_{hx}</span> depends on <span class=\"mi\">NTU = UA/C_{\\min}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{\\min}/C_{\\max}</span> and the flow arrangement. Counterflow is the most effective arrangement. Regenerative cooling jackets and the precoolers of combined-cycle engines (Chapter 44) are heat exchangers.</p>"}},"20":{"intro":"","secs":{"20.1":"<p>The traction on a surface with normal <span class=\"mi\">\\widehat{\\mathbf{n}}</span> is <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{t} = \\mathbf{\\sigma} \\cdot \\widehat{\\mathbf{n}}</span>, where the Cauchy stress tensor <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{\\sigma}</span> is symmetric. Its eigenvalues are the <em>principal stresses</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{1} \\geq \\sigma_{2} \\geq \\sigma_{3}</span>, acting on planes free of shear; the maximum shear stress is <span class=\"mi\">\\left( \\sigma_{1} - \\sigma_{3} \\right)/2</span>. In 2-D, Mohr’s circle has center <span class=\"mi\">\\left( \\sigma_{x} + \\sigma_{y} \\right)/2</span> and radius <span class=\"mi\">\\sqrt{\\left\\lbrack \\left( \\sigma_{x} - \\sigma_{y} \\right)/2 \\right\\rbrack^{2} + \\tau_{xy}^{2}}</span>. Equilibrium requires <span class=\"mi\">\\partial\\sigma_{ij}/\\partial x_{j} + f_{i} = 0</span>. For small displacements <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{u}</span>, the strain tensor is <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon_{ij} = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( \\partial_{j}u_{i} + \\partial_{i}u_{j} \\right)</span>.</p>","20.2":"<div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Isotropic linear elasticity with thermal strain:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon_{ij} = \\frac{1 + \\nu}{E}\\sigma_{ij} - \\frac{\\nu}{E}\\sigma_{kk}\\delta_{ij} + \\alpha\\Delta T\\,\\delta_{ij}</span>, with Young’s modulus <span class=\"mi\">E</span>, Poisson’s ratio <span class=\"mi\">\\nu</span>, shear modulus <span class=\"mi\">G = E/\\left\\lbrack 2(1 + \\nu) \\right\\rbrack</span>, bulk modulus <span class=\"mi\">K = E/\\left\\lbrack 3(1 - 2\\nu) \\right\\rbrack</span> and thermal expansion coefficient <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span>.</p></div><p>Composites are anisotropic: an orthotropic lamina needs nine constants (Chapter 33). Beyond yield, metals deform plastically; polymers and composite matrices are viscoelastic.</p>","20.3":"<p><em>Axial:</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma = P/A</span>, elongation <span class=\"mi\">\\delta = PL/(EA)</span>. <em>Torsion of a circular shaft:</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\tau = Tr/J</span>, twist <span class=\"mi\">\\phi = TL/(GJ)</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">J = \\pi d^{4}/32</span>. Thin-walled closed sections carry a shear flow <span class=\"mi\">q = T/\\left( 2A_{m} \\right)</span> (Bredt), where <span class=\"mi\">A_{m}</span> is the area enclosed by the wall midline. <em>Bending:</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma = - My/I</span> with <span class=\"mi\">I = bh^{3}/12</span> (rectangle), <span class=\"mi\">\\pi d^{4}/64</span> (solid circle) or <span class=\"mi\">\\pi r^{3}t</span> (thin tube). <em>Euler–Bernoulli beam:</em> <span class=\"mi\">EI\\, w⁗ = q(x)</span>; a cantilever with tip load <span class=\"mi\">P</span> deflects <span class=\"mi\">PL^{3}/(3EI)</span>.</p>","20.4":"<div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Thin-walled vessels</strong> (<span class=\"mi\">t/r &lt; 0.1</span>): cylinder hoop stress <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{\\theta} = pr/t</span>, axial stress <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{x} = pr/(2t)</span>; sphere <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma = pr/(2t)</span>. <strong>Minimum mass:</strong> for a sphere sized to allowable stress <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{a}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">m = \\frac{3}{2}pV\\rho/\\sigma_{a}</span>. Tank mass scales with <span class=\"mi\">pV</span> and inversely with the <em>specific strength</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{a}/\\rho</span>, independent of shape details.</p></div><p>This scaling governs propulsion architecture: pressure-fed stages need high tank pressures and therefore heavy tanks, while pump-fed stages keep tank pressures low (Chapter 27). Composite overwrapped pressure vessels (COPVs), with a thin metal liner wrapped in carbon fiber, store high-pressure helium efficiently but bring their own failure modes.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Physical hazard</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> A pressurized vessel stores energy that is released explosively if it ruptures. A gas-filled vessel stores far more energy than a liquid-filled one at the same pressure; Chapter 54 gives the formula and a worked number.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Improvised pressure vessels and pneumatic (gas) proof tests have killed and injured experimenters, students included.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> Proof tests are done hydrostatically (with liquid), behind barriers, by trained staff. Study the ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code and pressure-system safety courses offered by universities and professional bodies. Never pressurize an untested, modified or improvised container.</p></aside>","20.5":"<p>Slender compressed members fail by instability below their material strength. An Euler column has <span class=\"mi\">P_{cr} = \\pi^{2}EI/(KL)^{2}</span>, with effective-length factor <span class=\"mi\">K = 1</span> (pinned–pinned), 0.5 (fixed–fixed), 0.7 (fixed–pinned) or 2 (cantilever). A flat plate has <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{cr} = k\\pi^{2}E(t/b)^{2}/\\left\\lbrack 12\\left( 1 - \\nu^{2} \\right) \\right\\rbrack</span>. A thin cylinder in axial compression has the classical value</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\sigma_{cl} = \\frac{E}{\\sqrt{3\\left( 1 - \\nu^{2} \\right)}}\\frac{t}{R} \\approx 0.605\\, E\\frac{t}{R}\\ (\\nu = 0.3),</span></p><p>but real shells buckle at a fraction of this because of geometric imperfections. Design therefore uses empirical <em>knockdown factors</em> (Chapter 33). Internal pressure stiffens thin shells, which is why lightweight tanks are often pressurized before carrying flight loads.</p>","20.6":"<p><em>Ductile yielding:</em> von Mises predicts yield when <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{vm} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\left\\lbrack \\left( \\sigma_{1} - \\sigma_{2} \\right)^{2} + \\left( \\sigma_{2} - \\sigma_{3} \\right)^{2} + \\left( \\sigma_{3} - \\sigma_{1} \\right)^{2} \\right\\rbrack}</span> reaches <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{y}</span>; Tresca uses the maximum shear. <em>Fracture mechanics:</em> a crack of size <span class=\"mi\">a</span> under stress <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma</span> has stress-intensity factor <span class=\"mi\">K_{I} = Y\\sigma\\sqrt{\\pi a}</span> (<span class=\"mi\">Y</span> is a geometry factor of order 1), and fracture occurs when <span class=\"mi\">K_{I} = K_{IC}</span> (fracture toughness), giving a critical crack size <span class=\"mi\">a_{c} = \\left( K_{IC}/Y\\sigma \\right)^{2}/\\pi</span>. Pressure vessels are designed to <em>leak before burst</em>: a crack should penetrate the wall before reaching critical length. <em>Fatigue:</em> cyclic stress causes failure after <span class=\"mi\">N</span> cycles described by S–N curves; mean stress reduces life (Goodman correction). Miner’s rule estimates cumulative damage as <span class=\"mi\">D = \\sum n_{i}/N_{i}</span>, with failure expected near <span class=\"mi\">D = 1</span>. Crack growth follows the Paris law <span class=\"mi\">da/dN = C(\\Delta K)^{m}</span>. <em>Creep:</em> above roughly <span class=\"mi\">0.4T_{m}</span>, strain grows with time under constant load. The Larson–Miller parameter <span class=\"mi\">P = T\\left( C + logt_{r} \\right)</span> collapses stress-rupture data.</p>","20.7":"<p>A bar fully restrained against expansion develops <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma = - E\\alpha\\Delta T</span>; a plate restrained in two directions, <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma = - E\\alpha\\Delta T/(1 - \\nu)</span>. Through-thickness temperature gradients cause bending stresses even without external restraint. The <em>thermal shock resistance</em> <span class=\"mi\">R = \\sigma_{f}(1 - \\nu)/(E\\alpha)</span> ranks materials for sudden heating. Engineering remedies: allow free expansion (sliding joints, flexible standoffs), match expansion coefficients at joints, and grade material properties. These issues dominate hot structures and thermal protection (Chapter 42).</p>","20.8":"<p>Crystal structure governs ductility: face-centered cubic metals (Al, Ni, Cu) stay ductile at cryogenic temperatures; body-centered cubic refractory metals (W, Mo, Nb) have a ductile-to-brittle transition. Strength comes from solid solution, precipitates (Al alloys; the γ′ phase in nickel superalloys), fine grains and cold work.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Material (representative, room temperature)</th> <th><span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{\\rho}</span> (kg/m³)</th> <th><span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{E}</span> (GPa)</th> <th><span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{\\sigma}_{\\mathbf{y}}</span> (MPa)</th> <th>Typical use</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Al 2219-T87</td> <td>2840</td> <td>74</td> <td>≈ 395</td> <td>Cryogenic tanks</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Al–Li 2195-T8</td> <td>2710</td> <td>76</td> <td>≈ 550</td> <td>Lightweight cryogenic tanks</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Ti-6Al-4V</td> <td>4430</td> <td>114</td> <td>≈ 880</td> <td>Pressure vessels, structures to ≈ 400 °C</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Inconel 718</td> <td>8190</td> <td>200</td> <td>≈ 1100</td> <td>Turbopumps, manifolds to ≈ 650 °C</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Copper alloys (e.g. GRCop)</td> <td>≈ 8800</td> <td>≈ 110</td> <td>≈ 200</td> <td>Regeneratively cooled chamber liners</td> </tr> <tr> <td>CFRP (quasi-isotropic)</td> <td>≈ 1600</td> <td>50–70</td> <td>500–700 (ultimate)</td> <td>Interstages, fairings, COPVs</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>Values are representative only; design uses statistically based allowables (for example MMPDS). Special environments: <em>hydrogen embrittlement</em> degrades high-strength steels and some nickel alloys; <em>oxygen compatibility</em> matters because many metals ignite in high-pressure oxygen, which drives the choice of nickel alloys and coatings in oxygen-rich turbines; composites microcrack and leak at cryogenic temperatures. Ceramics are strong at high temperature but brittle, and their strength scatter follows Weibull statistics (Chapter 42).</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Material indices (Ashby).</strong> Minimum mass for a given function: tie in tension, maximize <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma/\\rho</span>; stiff beam, <span class=\"mi\">E^{1/2}/\\rho</span>; stiff panel, <span class=\"mi\">E^{1/3}/\\rho</span>; pressure vessel, <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma/\\rho</span>. Plotting materials on log axes of the two properties turns selection into drawing a line of constant index.</p></div>"}},"21":{"intro":"","secs":{"21.1":"<p>Coulomb’s law: <span class=\"mi\">F = q_{1}q_{2}/\\left( 4\\pi\\varepsilon_{0}r^{2} \\right)</span> with <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon_{0} = 8.854 \\times 10^{- 12}</span> F/m. The field <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{E} = \\mathbf{F}/q</span> derives from a potential, <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{E} = - \\nabla V</span>. Gauss’s law: <span class=\"mi\">\\oint\\mathbf{E} \\cdot d\\mathbf{A} = Q_{enc}/\\varepsilon_{0}</span>. Capacitance is <span class=\"mi\">C = Q/V</span>; a parallel-plate capacitor has <span class=\"mi\">C = \\varepsilon A/d</span> and stores energy <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{1}{2}CV^{2}</span>.</p>","21.2":"<p>Current <span class=\"mi\">I = dQ/dt</span>; Ohm’s law <span class=\"mi\">V = IR</span>; power <span class=\"mi\">P = IV = I^{2}R</span>. Kirchhoff’s laws: currents into a node sum to zero, and voltages around a loop sum to zero. An RC circuit has time constant <span class=\"mi\">\\tau = RC</span> and step response <span class=\"mi\">1 - e^{- t/\\tau}</span>; an RL circuit has <span class=\"mi\">\\tau = L/R</span>. A series RLC circuit is a second-order system (Chapter 11) with <span class=\"mi\">\\omega_{n} = 1/\\sqrt{LC}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\zeta = (R/2)\\sqrt{C/L}</span>. In AC analysis, impedances are <span class=\"mi\">Z_{R} = R</span>, <span class=\"mi\">Z_{C} = 1/(j\\omega C)</span> and <span class=\"mi\">Z_{L} = j\\omega L</span> (engineers write <span class=\"mi\">j</span> for <span class=\"mi\">\\sqrt{- 1}</span>), so circuits are solved algebraically with phasors.</p>","21.3":"<div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Lorentz force:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{F} = q\\left( \\mathbf{E} + \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B} \\right)</span>. A charge in a uniform field <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{B}</span> gyrates at the cyclotron frequency <span class=\"mi\">\\omega_{c} = |q|B/m</span> with radius <span class=\"mi\">r_{L} = mv_{\\bot}/\\left( |q|B \\right)</span>. A wire experiences <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{F} = I\\mathbf{L} \\times \\mathbf{B}</span>.</p></div><p>Ampère’s law: <span class=\"mi\">\\oint\\mathbf{B} \\cdot d\\mathbf{l} = \\mu_{0}I_{enc}</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">\\mu_{0} = 4\\pi \\times 10^{- 7}</span> H/m; a long solenoid has <span class=\"mi\">B = \\mu_{0}nI</span>. Faraday’s law: the induced EMF is <span class=\"mi\">- d\\Phi_{B}/dt</span> (Lenz’s law gives the sign). Electric motors drive actuators and electric turbopumps; generators and alternators supply vehicle power.</p>","21.4":"<p><span class=\"md\">\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{E} = \\frac{\\rho_{q}}{\\varepsilon_{0}},\\quad\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf{B} = 0,\\quad\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{E} = - \\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{B}}{\\partial t},\\quad\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{B} = \\mu_{0}\\mathbf{J} + \\mu_{0}\\varepsilon_{0}\\frac{\\partial\\mathbf{E}}{\\partial t}.</span></p><p>In vacuum these give waves traveling at <span class=\"mi\">c = 1/\\sqrt{\\mu_{0}\\varepsilon_{0}}</span>, with wavelength <span class=\"mi\">\\lambda = c/f</span>. The Poynting vector <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{S} = \\mathbf{E} \\times \\mathbf{H}</span> is the power flux. Waves cannot penetrate a plasma at frequencies below its plasma frequency, the cause of entry communication blackout (Chapter 48).</p>","21.5":"<p>An antenna’s gain <span class=\"mi\">G</span> concentrates radiated power in direction; its effective aperture is <span class=\"mi\">A_{e} = G\\lambda^{2}/(4\\pi)</span>, and a dish of diameter <span class=\"mi\">D</span> has <span class=\"mi\">G = \\eta(\\pi D/\\lambda)^{2}</span>. Between isotropic antennas, power falls by the <em>free-space path loss</em> <span class=\"mi\">(4\\pi d/\\lambda)^{2}</span>. Logarithmic units: <span class=\"mi\">dB = 10\\log_{10}\\left( P_{2}/P_{1} \\right) = 20\\log_{10}\\left( V_{2}/V_{1} \\right)</span>; dBW and dBm are referenced to 1 W and 1 mW. The effective isotropic radiated power is EIRP = <span class=\"mi\">P_{t}G_{t}</span>. Link budgets add and subtract these decibel quantities (Chapter 34).</p>","21.6":"<p>Diodes conduct one way; transistors (BJT, MOSFET) act as switches or amplifiers. <em>Ideal op-amp rules</em> (with negative feedback): no current enters the inputs and the two inputs sit at the same voltage. Hence an inverting amplifier has gain <span class=\"mi\">- R_{f}/R_{in}</span> and a non-inverting one <span class=\"mi\">1 + R_{f}/R_{g}</span>. An <span class=\"mi\">N</span>-bit analog-to-digital converter resolves <span class=\"mi\">2^{N}</span> levels; for a full-scale sine its ideal quantization-limited signal-to-noise ratio is <span class=\"mi\">6.02N + 1.76</span> dB.</p>","21.7":"<div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Sampling theorem.</strong> A band-limited signal is recoverable from samples taken at <span class=\"mi\">f_{s} &gt; 2f_{\\max}</span>. A component at frequency <span class=\"mi\">f</span> above <span class=\"mi\">f_{s}/2</span> appears falsely at the alias <span class=\"mi\">\\left| f - kf_{s} \\right|</span> for the integer <span class=\"mi\">k</span> that brings it into <span class=\"mi\">\\left\\lbrack 0,f_{s}/2 \\right\\rbrack</span>. An analog <em>anti-aliasing</em> low-pass filter must precede the converter, because aliasing cannot be undone afterward.</p></div><p>A first-order RC low-pass filter has cutoff <span class=\"mi\">f_{c} = 1/(2\\pi RC)</span> and rolls off at 20 dB/decade. Digital filters are FIR (always stable, can have linear phase) or IIR (efficient, can be unstable). Thermal noise power in bandwidth <span class=\"mi\">B</span> is <span class=\"mi\">kTB</span>, which is −174 dBm/Hz at 290 K. Receiver quality is expressed by the noise figure, or the system noise temperature.</p>","21.8":"<p>Lithium-ion cells store roughly 150–270 Wh/kg; multi-junction solar cells convert about 30% of sunlight (1361 W/m² at 1 AU). Power budgets sum loads by mode, including converter efficiencies, harness losses and margin. Electric-pump-fed engines (Chapter 27) and electric propulsion (Chapter 48) are limited by battery or array specific power.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Physical hazard</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Lithium-ion cells can enter thermal runaway, producing fire and toxic gas, when overcharged, short-circuited, crushed or overheated. Vehicle electrical systems and ground equipment can carry lethal voltages and currents.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Battery and electrical failures are a common cause of laboratory fires and of test-site incidents.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> University electronics laboratories and battery-safety training; manufacturers’ charge, discharge and temperature limits; protection circuits and fused wiring; electrical-safety training (lockout/tagout) before working on powered hardware.</p></aside>","21.9":"<p>A <em>strain gauge</em> changes resistance as <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta R/R = GF \\cdot \\varepsilon</span> (gauge factor about 2) and is read in a Wheatstone bridge. <em>Thermocouples</em> generate a Seebeck voltage (type K about 41 μV/K) and need cold-junction compensation. Resistance temperature detectors are accurate at moderate temperature. Pressure transducers may be piezoresistive (static and dynamic) or piezoelectric (dynamic only). Every measurement passes through a chain of sensor, signal conditioning (excitation, amplification, filtering), conversion, processing and recording. Each link contributes uncertainty (Chapter 13) and must be calibrated against traceable standards.</p>"}},"22":{"intro":"","secs":{"22.1":"<p>Feedback compares a measured output with the desired value and acts on the error. It reduces sensitivity to plant uncertainty, rejects disturbances, and can stabilize an unstable plant. The costs are injection of sensor noise and the possibility of making a stable plant unstable. With loop transfer function <span class=\"mi\">L = GC</span> (plant <span class=\"mi\">G</span>, controller <span class=\"mi\">C</span>, unity feedback), the closed loop is <span class=\"mi\">T = L/(1 + L)</span>, the <em>sensitivity</em> is <span class=\"mi\">S = 1/(1 + L)</span>, and <span class=\"mi\">S + T = 1</span>. Good tracking at low frequency needs <span class=\"mi\">|L| \\gg 1</span> there; good noise rejection at high frequency needs <span class=\"mi\">|L| \\ll 1</span> there.</p>","22.2":"<p><span class=\"mi\">G(s) = N(s)/D(s)</span>: zeros are roots of <span class=\"mi\">N</span> and poles are roots of <span class=\"mi\">D</span>. A linear time-invariant system is (bounded-input bounded-output) stable if and only if every pole has a negative real part. The <em>Routh–Hurwitz</em> array decides this from the coefficients of the characteristic polynomial without solving for the roots. For example, <span class=\"mi\">s^{3} + a_{2}s^{2} + a_{1}s + a_{0}</span> is stable if and only if all coefficients are positive and <span class=\"mi\">a_{2}a_{1} &gt; a_{0}</span>.</p>","22.3":"<p>For the canonical second-order system <span class=\"mi\">\\omega_{n}^{2}/\\left( s^{2} + 2\\zeta\\omega_{n}s + \\omega_{n}^{2} \\right)</span>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p>Overshoot <span class=\"mi\">M_{p} = exp\\left( - \\pi\\zeta/\\sqrt{1 - \\zeta^{2}} \\right)</span>; 2% settling time <span class=\"mi\">t_{s} \\approx 4/\\left( \\zeta\\omega_{n} \\right)</span>; rise time <span class=\"mi\">t_{r} \\approx 1.8/\\omega_{n}</span>. Steady-state error to a step is zero if the loop contains at least one integrator (“type 1”). In general, use the final value theorem.</p></div>","22.4":"<p>As a gain <span class=\"mi\">K</span> in <span class=\"mi\">1 + KG_{0}(s) = 0</span> rises from 0 to ∞, the closed-loop poles move from the open-loop poles to the open-loop zeros, or to infinity along asymptotes at angles <span class=\"mi\">(2k + 1)180^{\\circ}/(n - m)</span> centered at <span class=\"mi\">(\\sum p - \\sum z)/(n - m)</span>. Real-axis segments lie to the left of an odd number of real poles and zeros. The locus shows at a glance which gains are stable and how damping varies with gain.</p>","22.5":"<p><span class=\"mi\">L(j\\omega)</span> has a magnitude and a phase; Bode plots draw <span class=\"mi\">20\\log|L|</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\angle L</span> against <span class=\"mi\">\\log\\omega</span>, with each pole contributing −20 dB/decade and up to −90°. <em>Nyquist criterion:</em> the number of unstable closed-loop poles is <span class=\"mi\">Z = N + P</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">N</span> is the number of clockwise encirclements of −1 by <span class=\"mi\">L(j\\omega)</span> and <span class=\"mi\">P</span> the number of unstable open-loop poles.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Stability margins.</strong> <em>Gain margin</em>: the factor by which gain can rise before instability, read where the phase is −180°. <em>Phase margin</em> (PM): <span class=\"mi\">180^{\\circ} + \\angle L</span> at the crossover frequency where <span class=\"mi\">|L| = 1</span>. For moderately damped loops, <span class=\"mi\">\\zeta \\approx PM/100^{\\circ}</span>. A pure time delay <span class=\"mi\">T</span> multiplies <span class=\"mi\">L</span> by <span class=\"mi\">e^{- sT}</span>, adding phase lag <span class=\"mi\">\\omega T</span> with no change in gain. Delay therefore always erodes phase margin.</p></div>","22.6":"<p><span class=\"mi\">u = K_{p}e + K_{i}\\int e\\, dt + K_{d}\\, de/dt</span>. The proportional term sets stiffness, the integral term removes steady error, and the derivative term adds damping (phase lead). The derivative must be filtered, for example <span class=\"mi\">K_{d}s/\\left( \\tau_{f}s + 1 \\right)</span>, to limit noise amplification. When the actuator saturates, the integrator keeps accumulating error (<em>windup</em>) and causes large overshoot; anti-windup clamps the integrator or back-calculates it from the saturated output.</p>","22.7":"<p>A <em>lead</em> compensator <span class=\"mi\">(s/z + 1)/(s/p + 1)</span> with <span class=\"mi\">p &gt; z</span> adds phase near crossover to improve the margins. A <em>lag</em> compensator raises low-frequency gain to reduce steady error. A <em>notch</em> filter suppresses a narrow band, such as a structural bending mode (Chapter 32).</p>","22.8":"<p>With <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{\\mathbf{x}} = \\mathbf{Ax} + \\mathbf{Bu}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{Cx} + \\mathbf{Du}</span>, the transfer matrix is <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{G}(s) = \\mathbf{C}\\left( s\\mathbf{I} - \\mathbf{A} \\right)^{- 1}\\mathbf{B} + \\mathbf{D}</span>. If the <em>controllability matrix</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\left\\lbrack \\mathbf{B}\\ \\mathbf{AB}\\ \\cdots\\ \\mathbf{A}^{n - 1}\\mathbf{B} \\right\\rbrack</span> has full rank, state feedback <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{u} = - \\mathbf{Kx}</span> can place the closed-loop eigenvalues anywhere. If the <em>observability matrix</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\left\\lbrack \\mathbf{C};\\ \\mathbf{CA};\\ \\ldots \\right\\rbrack</span> has full rank, an observer <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}}} = \\mathbf{A}\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}} + \\mathbf{Bu} + \\mathbf{L}\\left( \\mathbf{y} - \\mathbf{C}\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}} \\right)</span> can reconstruct the state. By the separation principle (for linear models) the two can be designed independently. Chapter 46 makes both optimal (LQR, Kalman filter).</p>","22.9":"<p>Controllers run in discrete time with period <span class=\"mi\">T</span>. A zero-order hold adds an effective delay of about <span class=\"mi\">T/2</span>, plus any computation delay. The <span class=\"mi\">z</span>-transform (<span class=\"mi\">z = e^{sT}</span>) maps the stable left half-plane into the unit disk <span class=\"mi\">|z| &lt; 1</span>. Continuous designs are discretized, for example by Tustin’s substitution <span class=\"mi\">s \\leftarrow (2/T)(z - 1)/(z + 1)</span>. Rule of thumb: sample at least 10–30 times the closed-loop bandwidth, and account for the delay in the phase margin.</p>","22.10":"<p>A simplified rigid-body pitch model (no wind, small angles, angle of attack ≈ attitude error) is <span class=\"mi\">\\ddot{\\theta} = M_{\\alpha}\\theta + M_{\\delta}\\delta</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">\\delta</span> is the engine gimbal angle. If the center of pressure is ahead of the CG, <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\alpha} &gt; 0</span> and the open-loop poles <span class=\"mi\">\\pm \\sqrt{M_{\\alpha}}</span> include an unstable one. With PD control <span class=\"mi\">\\delta = - K_{p}\\theta - K_{d}\\dot{\\theta}</span>:</p><p><span class=\"md\">s^{2} + M_{\\delta}K_{d}s + \\left( M_{\\delta}K_{p} - M_{\\alpha} \\right) = 0.</span></p><p>Stability requires <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\delta}K_{p} &gt; M_{\\alpha}</span>, a <em>minimum</em> gain. A <em>maximum</em> gain is set by actuator bandwidth, sensor noise, time delay and flexible bending modes. The design must fit between these limits at every flight condition, which is why gains are scheduled with dynamic pressure and why notch filters and slosh models appear in real autopilots (Chapter 32).</p>"}}}});